 of engagement  for a multi-faith society but I think  you need that comment space and you need  those common rules of engagement I think  thirdly that in practice what this then  means is an attitude that is what i  would call open rather than closed and  that what's more it should be a goal of  public policy to encourage or facilitate  that openness there's an interesting  side consequence of this which is it  actually has an impact on globalization  as well on on your economic policy  because it's very hard to have an a  society that is open in terms of faith  in terms of culture in terms of  background if it's closed economically  so there is a link in fact between the  idea that in the modern world human  capital is  that human capital should be on the  basis of the equal dignity of each human  being and actually a successful economy  where people are recognizing that they  live in a world in which this is going  to be trade between nations exchange of  intellectual human and financial capital  and actually one of the reasons why  protectionism in my view is not a good  response to today's world is partly  because if you take that closed economic  attitude it actually becomes quite hard  to have an open attitude to two people  of different faiths and backgrounds  within society so in practice that  requires an open and not a closed  attitude that should be a goal of Public  Policy and here's one other thing which  i think is really important is that if  you have at that open attitude then I  think it doesn't really work unless it  comes on the basis of that openness  being there available for all and not  simply for a few so there is something  universal about that for it to work now  fourthly even if you believe you have an  exclusive truth faith truth you've still  got to be then supportive of an  inclusive society this is a point the  mirror slappers often often made and i  think it's it's worth just spending a  moment on which is that what he says in  essence that i understand it is is this  that look if you have a faith and lets  you view you're a christian you believe  in salvation through Jesus Christ you  cannot at a certain point I mean that is  that is what you believe is the truth  you cannot at a certain point say well  but I think you who don't believe that  have equal validity because  I mean I obviously that's the the whole  essence of your belief and so miroslav I  think has drawn a distinction between as  it were exclusivity in terms of this is  my my truth my faith truth that I  believe in and then using that as  exclusivity in terms of shutting out the  other person right and I think that is  true but i also think something else  that is interesting about the way  globalization interplays with with with  faith is that as that as that knowledge  deepens and as you learn to inhabit  freely that common space and as that  produces what I would say is a more open  attitude I think faith also evolved in  that process so that so that yes you  still believe in your own faith but your  openness to someone of another faith his  is more than just accepting that they  have that faith and that they believe  it's valid you also start to accept  their spiritual sincerity right even if  you don't necessarily accept their truth  claim and that's quite important and  you're getting some Paul earlier but of  course the extraordinary thing about the  early Christian church was simple going  out and saying this is a message that i  have for greek as well as do that this  is as it were a message that is  universal are not simply restricted to  to to the Jewish people when you come to  islam the whole point about what the  Prophet was saying in a way was to say  you actually have to push it even  broader than the up to then i have the  Jewish faith or the Christian faith had  acknowledged my point is simply  is that the fourth thing I think you  require for and this world of faith and  globalization to work is that at the  least you even if you believe you have  the exclusive truth you've got to want  an inclusive society but I also think  that you need to accept that there is a  level of respect that you have for the  person of a different faith then is more  than simply accepting as a fact that  they have a different faith but you also  accept the sincerity with which they  hold that faith and that and that some  good can come out of it in other words  you know when I engage with somebody of  a different faith who's engaged with us  in our projects for example in Africa on  the anti malaria campaign we work with  with I mean primarily actually with with  Jews and Muslims as well as Christians  hello actually there are Hindus involved  in it as well but when I see what  difference let's say and somebody of the  Jewish faith what difference their faith  mates to them and their actions it's  more than for me simply will I accept  they happen to feel very strongly and  I'm prepared to accept that reality I'm  actually accepting something more than  that I'm accepting that there is a  sincerity about their view and a  motivation as a result of that for them  to do good that sits alongside if you  like the issue to do with who is right  and wrong in a face sense but sits  alongside it quite powerfully you know I  mean so it's it's a this is what I think  is fascinating about the way the world  then developed because yes you keep your  own religious faith and you keep that  strongly but you're very openness to the  other and you're working alongside them  and you're  understanding of why it is that they are  as they are also changes your own sense  of what faith is is is about it doesn't  change your own belief but it does let  you acknowledge somewhat of a different  belief I think at a far more profound  way than simply accepting as a matter of  fact they've got a different faith and  that's where you move I think from  tolerance to respect which is a very  very important part of the journey now  the final thing is is is this and here  is where I think the debate is in the  sense that it at its most interesting  because we've really discussed this very  often for obvious reasons in the context  of what in an era of globalization how  do you make religious faith end up as a  source of coexistence or not and not  that complex so we really am we've  really looked at that the issue of faith  more in terms of of is it a force for  good or ill in faith terms or in social  terms there's something else though that  is is really interesting which is that  globalization is a force but if it is to  be welcomed it needs for the benefits of  that globalization to be shared for the  opportunities not to be restricted and  globalization without values governing  it and underpinning it globalization is  less likely to be accepted less likely  to be successful and actually less  likely to work because if it's not  accepted and it's not successful  globalization itself can become a force  that is that can produce conflict or or  ill and that's why therefore what is  interesting about the role of faith and  why people thought maybe you'd squeeze  faith out of society  as economic prosperity advanced why  isn't it happened in the end it hasn't  happened because of something very deep  within people that says I understand the  benefits of globalization I desire the  material prosperity that may come with  it but I know that in and of itself  there has to be something more in order  to make life worth living right and what  is more that as this globalization which  is in some ways an impersonal force  changes and disrupts people's lives so  there is a desire to reassert some sense  of values some sense of shaping this  process of globalization two ends that  we consider compatible with progress in  humanity so I think the interesting  thing in the end as miroslav was saying  earlier and and Doug was remarking about  the point of which people have religious  faith come to 